WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.170
ERICA CHUONG: Hi, everyone.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.460
I'm Erica Chuong, a partner
engineer at Google Cloud.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.790
Welcome back to Google Cloud
Technical Guides for Startups,

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.550
where we're creating a series of
videos for technical enablement

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.250
to help startups set up their
own Google Cloud platform

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.830
environment and grow their
businesses successfully

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.600
and sustainably on Google Cloud.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.780
This episode focuses on
setting up billing and alerts

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.180
and optimizing costs on
Google Cloud, which I know

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.980
is everyone's favorite topic.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.670
The good news is Google Cloud
makes billing management

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.710
and optimization really easy.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:43.310
In this video, we're going to
cover Google Cloud resources,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.500
billing administration,
discounting,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.060
and additional resources.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.010
So without any
further ado, let's

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.490
get right on into exploring the
Google Cloud platform together.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.260
If you're new to
Google Cloud, please

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.360
refer to video 2 of
the series, which

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.180
discusses Google Cloud
resources, organizations,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.083
and permissions.

00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:02.000
These concepts will be
important when it comes

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.770
to billing administration.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.590
A billing admin is responsible
for much more than just

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.540
making sure invoices get paid.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.240
They're also responsible for
managing payment instruments,

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.090
configuring billing exports,
viewing cost information,

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.460
and managing user roles
on the billing account.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.130
The way Cloud computing
is charged and billed,

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.680
it's a little of a shift
in mindset from what

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.630
most companies are used to.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:26.030
Instead of one-and-done
data center purchases,

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.910
billing is done monthly
and is consumption-based.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.320
Managing cost becomes
a little bit different,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.950
which we'll get into when we
discuss budgets and alerts.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.500
In order to connect a
project to a billing account,

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.600
you need to have the
correct permissions granted

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.420
on both the project and
billing account levels.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.090
The minimum roles required
include billing account user

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.060
on the billing account and
project billing manager

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.550
on the project or
resource associated.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.350
Higher permissions,
such as project owner,

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.450
will work as well but
are unnecessary due

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.123
to least-privilege design.

00:01:55.123 --> 00:01:56.540
While on the topic
of permissions,

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.940
it's important to note that
when a new billing account is

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:02.360
created, the user creating it is
usually also given the payment

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.197
profile admin role on
the payment profile.

00:02:05.197 --> 00:02:07.280
If you do need to change
the billing account admin

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.139
role to someone else
for whatever reason,

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:12.450
remember to also change the
payment profile admin rule.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.230
So what is a billing
account specifically?

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.410
A billing account is essentially
how you pay for projects.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.840
Projects can exist
without a billing account

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.540
thanks to the free tier that
many Google products have,

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.430
but you're limited to quotas.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.980
To fully operate, a project
must have a billing account

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.480
associated to it.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.330
A billing account is just where
costs and charges are bucketed.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.330
And this also represents a
customer's legal relationship

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.200
with Google.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.190
The billing account is then both
a legal and financial entity--

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.440
an organization compared
to billing account

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.200
with the organization and
contractual obligation

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.410
between Google and the customer
exists at the billing account

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:49.280
level.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.990
There are two types
of billing accounts.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:52.920
There's invoiced or offline.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.210
And that just refers to accounts
that are invoiced on net terms,

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.840
typically 30 days, and have
a signed contract associated.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.480
There's also
self-serve or online,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.340
and that refers to
accounts that sign up

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.020
in a self-service
manner with a credit

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.910
card, debit card,
or bank transfer

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:10.250
and are charged when
payment becomes due.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.400
Most organizations will have
a single billing account,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.230
but some may have multiple.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:16.850
Generally, if you
have multiple, it's

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:19.130
due to legal or
accounting reasons,

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.920
or maybe they need to pay
in multiple currencies.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.530
Something to keep in mind is
that having multiple billing

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.630
accounts can mean
additional overhead

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.820
and could make things
tricky to track and manage

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.240
depending on how many you have.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.030
So just be cognizant
of that before you

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:34.040
start setting up multiple.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.090
A project could only be
assigned to one billing account,

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.020
but a billing account can be
tied to multiple projects.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.240
A billing account is managed
through the Google Cloud

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.680
Console.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.650
A billing account is specific
to Google Cloud resources,

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.420
but there is another resource
called a payments profile,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.380
which I mentioned earlier, which
is a general Google billing

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.500
resource and applicable for
other services such as Maps

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.610
and Ads.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.430
Your billing account is linked
to your payment profile.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.000
You can see and manage
everything at pay.google.com.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.620
This is essentially
just a collection

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.690
of payment information
such as mailing addresses

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:11.580
and forms of payments for
the organization as a whole.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.090
So let's take a look at what
this looks like in the Console

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:14.900
itself.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.269
This is the interface
of a billing account.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:18.860
The billing ID is at the top.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.880
This is automatically
generated for you.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.340
You have your billing admin.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:23.810
You can have multiple here.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.330
This person can add
additional admins and users

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.120
and assign permissions directly
on the billing account.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.910
Each project you create will
be linked to a billing account.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.983
And here you can also
see which projects

00:04:33.983 --> 00:04:35.900
are linked to that
particular billing account.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:37.400
When you create
a new project, it

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.817
will ask you which
billing account

00:04:38.817 --> 00:04:40.100
you'd like to associate.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:43.460
You can also suspend a project
to stop billing at any time.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:46.010
Something to keep in mind
is that projects and billing

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:49.280
accounts do not necessarily need
to be in the same organization.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.680
As long as the user has
the correct permissions

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.110
to set up the relationship
between the billing

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.430
account and a project,
they could be anywhere.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.550
So you could manage things
cross-organizationally as well.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.360
So now that you have your
resources and billing accounts

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.800
set up and running,
you may be wondering,

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.940
how do I see what
resources says I'm using

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.530
and where are my largest
costs coming from?

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.530
That's relatively easy,
and there are a few ways

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:12.560
that you can do this.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.502
So first, you have your Cloud
Console billing reports.

00:05:16.502 --> 00:05:18.460
These are going to give
you general information

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.340
on your spend, and they're
ready to go, ready-made.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:22.940
Examples here.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.330
Another option is
your monthly invoice.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:27.850
This will show you details
of consumption associated

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.030
with a billing account.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:31.480
Depending on how many
resources you've consumed,

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.220
your bill could
be quite lengthy.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:35.110
So it might be a
lot to sift through.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.780
Another option is to
build a customized billing

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.970
dashboard in
BigQuery, Data Studio,

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:41.870
or a BI tool of your choice.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.470
This will give you more
granular reporting, analysis,

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.410
and visibility and allow you to
really slice and dice your data

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:49.362
and derive insights from it.

00:05:49.362 --> 00:05:51.070
So let's take a look
at an example of one

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:54.070
of these dashboards
built in Data Studio.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:57.580
So a Data Studio
dashboard such as this one

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.930
can give you a more visual
view of your billing.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:04.140
This dashboard has multiple
different views to give you

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.040
different levels of detail.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.830
The first is going to
be our GCP cost summary.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.970
We can see we have year-to-date
costs, quarter-to-date costs,

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.170
month-to-date costs.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:16.470
We also see trends month over
month, change in dollars.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.780
You can also add visuals
such as pie charts

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.090
to allow you to easily
slice and dice your data

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:23.430
and see how much of each
service is contributing

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.960
to your overall bill.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.720
You can also filter
based on labels.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.640
In this particular
dashboard, we've

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:34.500
added granularity on the team,
Google Cloud platform service,

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.300
and environment levels.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.670
So environment could also just
be production and deployment.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:44.710
So let's take a look at
our cost-by-team view.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:48.930
So once this loads,
we can see that we

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:50.790
have four different teams.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:54.670
And with this, we can get
granularity pretty easily.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:58.050
So very quickly, I can see
that almost 50% of billing

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.890
is by the pso team, 33% is
by the engineering team,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:05.460
and 16% is by the
networking team.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.340
Over on the right-hand
side, we also

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:11.010
have a trend
analysis to which we

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:16.050
can see how our data is
moving over a time basis.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:17.800
At the top, we
also have filters.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:20.550
So if I wanted to filter by
date range, metric, project,

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.290
or GCP service, those
are options as well.

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.380
Again, slice and dice for data.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.560
The same views can be seen on
an environment basis as well.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:32.310
These labels were created
on the project level

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:35.980
and were included in the
queries used to export the data.

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:38.820
So currently, we only
have one environment.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.200
It's production in this case.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.880
But we can very easily see that
we've accrued a cost of $827.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:46.963
And same thing as
we saw before, we

00:07:46.963 --> 00:07:48.630
have a graph on the
right-hand side that

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.820
shows our trend analysis.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.590
This is actually
a premade template

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.475
that you can re-use with
your own billing export data.

00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:56.850
And there are
instructions on how

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:00.240
to do so within the
bottom tab right here.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.460
Next is managing
our monthly spend.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.240
So we really want to remain
on target to see where we are,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.400
and that's where budgets
and alerts come in.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:12.090
So alerts are important to avoid
any sort of billing surprises.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.980
And you can set up
multiple alert percentages

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.870
to get notifications as
usage continues to increase.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:18.420
So for example,
if I wanted to be

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.810
notified when I reached 50%,
75%, and 90% of my budget,

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.100
I can do so.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:25.440
You can set notifications
up in your email,

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.300
or you can set them
to automatically take

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.630
action using Pub/Sub, Cloud
Functions, or Cloud Run.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.760
This could include
shutting down a resource,

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:35.610
or changing your resource
usage, or sending notifications

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:37.650
to a ticketing system
such as ServiceNow.

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:39.669
There's also a forecast trigger.

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:41.429
So this will allow
you to be notified

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:44.220
as soon as you are
forecasted to reach a budget

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.810
by the end of the month.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.640
This allows you to
make changes to lower

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:51.060
your spend if needed and is
a much more proactive rather

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.910
than reactive response.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.780
So as mentioned when I showed
that Data Studio dashboard,

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:00.120
labels can help you manage
Cloud applications at scale.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.630
So they offer clarity
into resources being used

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:04.695
and can help deploy,
monitor, and manage

00:09:04.695 --> 00:09:06.950
at an individual
application level.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:10.820
They can give visibility into
usage by department or group

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:13.250
and help with cost control
and resource management.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:16.130
So we all love discounts
and optimization.

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:18.170
And here are just
a few ways to do so

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.250
within your GCP environments.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.210
So CUDs, or Committed
Use Discounts,

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:26.180
allow users to prebuy in bulk.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.310
Committed Use Discounts
are ideal for workloads

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:29.990
with predictable resource needs.

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.820
When you purchase a
Committed Use Discount,

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:33.920
you purchase Compute
Engine resources

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.120
such as vCPUs, memory, GPUs,
local SSDs, and sole tenant

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:40.400
nodes at a discounted
price in return

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.100
for committing to paying
for those resources for one

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:44.870
or three years.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:47.690
The discount is up to
57% for most resources

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.460
like machine types and GPUs.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:52.610
The discount can also
be up to a whopping 70%

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.913
for memory-optimized
machine types.

00:09:54.913 --> 00:09:56.705
For Cloud SQL, it's a
little bit different.

00:09:56.705 --> 00:09:58.940
You commit to a
consistent amount

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:02.120
of usage measured in dollars
per hour of equivalent on-demand

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.000
spend for a one or
three-year term.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.010
In exchange, you receive
a discounted rate

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:09.650
on the applicable usage
your commitment covers.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:11.900
So you can purchase
Committed Use Discounts

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:13.340
from any Cloud billing account.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:16.700
And the discount applies to any
eligible usage on projects paid

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:18.290
for by that billing account.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:22.460
Any sort of overages are then
charged at an on-demand rate.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:24.800
There's also Sustained
Use Discounts,

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:28.530
and these are one of GCPs larger
points of differentiation.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.490
So this is basically
a large discount

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:33.590
on Compute Engine
resources for workloads

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.470
that are run for a significant
portion of the month.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.540
There's also preemptible VMs.

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:40.400
And this is another
great way to lower costs

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:42.770
if you have workloads that
are fault-tolerant and can

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:45.200
withstand possible
instance preemptions.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.750
Discounts for these
can be up to 80%.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:49.337
I suggest talking
to your sales team

00:10:49.337 --> 00:10:51.170
to see how you can
optimize your environment

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:53.820
and really take advantage
of any applicable discounts.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:56.120
So with that, we have tons
of resources and guides

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:59.030
around setting up billing
on our documentation site.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:00.890
I highly encourage
you to take a look

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:03.320
prior to setting everything
up in your own environment.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:05.900
Should you run into any issues,
our billing support team

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:07.540
is always available as well.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:10.040
A couple of resources have been
added to the description box

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:11.900
below to help you
understand these concepts

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:12.870
in further detail.

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:14.250
So please check them out.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:15.980
This brings us to
the end of the video

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:19.400
where we covered Google Cloud
resources, billing admin,

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.860
discounting, and
additional resources.

